Category: Cloud Security | AWS | IAM | S3 | Shared Responsibility Model | Incident Analysis

Overview
This room introduces the AWS Shared Responsibility Model and explains how security responsibilities are divided between AWS and its customers.
AWS is responsible for securing the underlying cloud infrastructure, while customers are responsible for securing the services, identities, applications, and data they configure and deploy in the cloud. The exact division of responsibility depends on the cloud service model being used.
The room also examines real-world cloud security incidents to demonstrate how exposed credentials, overly permissive IAM policies, and misconfigured storage can lead to data exposure.
Task 1 - Introduction
AWS secures the cloud platform and services to a certain extent, while customers are responsible for securing what they build and configure on top of it.
Key takeaway: Security in the cloud is a shared responsibility. Using AWS does not automatically make applications, identities, or data secure.
Task 2 - Shared Responsibility Model Overview
The Shared Responsibility Model divides security responsibilities between the cloud provider and the customer. The customer’s responsibilities vary depending on the cloud service model.
Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)
Operating an in-house data center or server facility requires responsibility for physical protection, hardware reliability, system patching, network configuration, and many other tasks.
Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) provides virtualized computing resources over the internet. Organizations can provision virtual servers through a web console without managing the physical hardware or data center.
Examples include Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud. With IaaS, the provider manages the physical infrastructure, while the customer typically manages the operating system, network configuration, identity permissions, applications, and data.
Platform as a Service (PaaS)
Platform as a Service (PaaS) provides an environment for developing and deploying applications without requiring developers to manage the underlying servers directly.
Developers can focus on writing code and deploying applications, while the platform manages more of the hosting environment. Examples include Heroku, Vercel, and Google App Engine.
TryHackMe's cloud functionality illustrates how a platform can provide users with a simplified way to build and publish interactive rooms while relying on cloud infrastructure underneath.
Software as a Service (SaaS)
Software as a Service (SaaS) delivers cloud-hosted applications that users can access without installing or maintaining the underlying application infrastructure themselves.
Examples include Slack, Zoom, Gmail, Google Docs, and Dropbox. The provider manages most of the application stack, while customers remain responsible for appropriate account access, data handling, and configuration choices available to them.
Function as a Service (FaaS)
Function as a Service (FaaS), commonly associated with serverless computing, allows developers to deploy individual functions or pieces of code that run in response to events, such as HTTP requests, file uploads, or database updates.
For example, AWS Lambda manages the underlying infrastructure and execution environment, while the customer remains responsible for the function's code, permissions, secrets, and logging configuration.
