Task 7 - Conclusion
Many high-impact cloud incidents involve recurring security weaknesses, including:
- Over-permissive IAM policies that allow compromised identities to access more resources than necessary.
- Public exposure through S3 buckets, Security Groups, and endpoints.
- Leaked secrets such as access keys stored in source code.
- Insufficient logging and alerting that delays detection of suspicious activity.
The central lesson is that cloud security requires both provider-side infrastructure protection and customer-side security configuration. Strong IAM controls, protected secrets, restricted data access, and effective monitoring help reduce the likelihood and impact of cloud incidents.
Practical Security Checklist
- [ ] Apply least privilege to IAM users, roles, and policies.
- [ ] Avoid storing access keys or secrets in source code.
- [ ] Prefer IAM roles and short-lived credentials where appropriate.
- [ ] Enable MFA for sensitive accounts and actions.
- [ ] Enable S3 Block Public Access and review bucket policies.
- [ ] Monitor changes to IAM permissions and S3 access settings.
- [ ] Enable and centralize CloudTrail logs.
- [ ] Alert on unusual STS activity and unexpected data access.
- [ ] Review public endpoints and network exposure regularly.
Lessons Learned
The AWS Shared Responsibility Model clarifies that cloud providers secure the underlying infrastructure, while customers must protect their identities, applications, configurations, and data. Real-world incidents demonstrate how SSRF, exposed credentials, excessive IAM permissions, and public S3 access can turn a configuration weakness into a major data breach. Applying least privilege, protecting secrets, blocking unintended public access, and monitoring cloud activity are essential practices for reducing cloud security risk.
